      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-31643",
      ":question/problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e/2$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{1/2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\sqrt{u}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{1}-\\sqrt{0}) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
